From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Stockdale <jstockdale@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 17:03:16 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] Input: nt36536: injected pen buttons follow the tool's
 proximity

The barrel button of this pen arrives over Bluetooth and is written into
the driver by the bridge daemon, which reported it at once in a frame of
its own, whether or not the pen was in range. libinput releases a
tool's buttons when the tool leaves proximity and takes no button event
while it is out, so a press or a release delivered then is lost or
mistimed. The pen's link also drops a release now and then, or sends a
code the bridge did not know (a press at 17:02:07 with no release before
the next press four seconds later; a release reported as 04 instead of
03, 2026-11-04): the driver then re-asserted "stylus button held" in
every frame, a press arrived with every proximity-in, and the compositor
kept the grab state of that episode for later touches.

Report an injected button at once only while the tool is in proximity;
out of range the change waits for the first frame that brings the pen
back, which the frame path already carries. The deferred proximity-out
releases the buttons in the kernel's state and clears the injected
state, so a lost release cannot outlive a lift; a button still held
comes back with the next press the pen reports.

Signed-off-by: John Stockdale <jstockdale@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36536/nt36xxx.c | 25 +++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36536/nt36xxx.c b/drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36536/nt36xxx.c
index cd008ca54778..6d28f8b06948 100644
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36536/nt36xxx.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36536/nt36xxx.c
@@ -336,8 +336,19 @@ static ssize_t pen_input_store(struct device *dev, struct device_attribute *attr
 		pen_inject.valid = true;
 	} else if (!strcmp(word, "button") && n == 3 && a >= 1 && a <= 2) {
 		pen_inject.btn[a - 1] = !!b;
-		input_report_key(ts->pen_input_dev, a == 1 ? BTN_STYLUS : BTN_STYLUS2, !!b);
-		input_sync(ts->pen_input_dev);
+		/*
+		 * At once only while the tool is in proximity.  Out of range the
+		 * change waits for the first frame that brings the pen back (the
+		 * frame path reports the injected state every frame): libinput
+		 * releases a tool's buttons when it leaves proximity and takes no
+		 * button event while it is out, so a press or release delivered
+		 * then is lost or mistimed - a release lost that way leaves the
+		 * compositor holding the stylus button for every later touch.
+		 */
+		if (ts->pen_in_prox) {
+			input_report_key(ts->pen_input_dev, a == 1 ? BTN_STYLUS : BTN_STYLUS2, !!b);
+			input_sync(ts->pen_input_dev);
+		}
 	} else {
 		mutex_unlock(&ts->lock);
 		return -EINVAL;
@@ -1214,6 +1225,16 @@ static void nvt_pen_out_work(struct work_struct *work)
 		data->pen_out_pending = false;
 		data->pen_in_prox = false;
 		input_report_abs(data->pen_input_dev, ABS_DISTANCE, 0);
+		/*
+		 * The buttons go with the tool, in the kernel's state and in the
+		 * injected one: the pen's Bluetooth link drops a release now and
+		 * then (or sends a code the bridge does not know), and a press that
+		 * outlived a lift would come back as a press at every approach.
+		 */
+		pen_inject.btn[0] = false;
+		pen_inject.btn[1] = false;
+		input_report_key(data->pen_input_dev, BTN_STYLUS, 0);
+		input_report_key(data->pen_input_dev, BTN_STYLUS2, 0);
 		input_report_key(data->pen_input_dev, BTN_TOOL_PEN, 0);
 		input_sync(data->pen_input_dev);
 	}
-- 
